EXT. COMMUNITY POOL, DIVING PLATFORM - LATE AFTERNOON

Chlorine light, gold on blue. The ten-meter platform stands over the water like a lighthouse in swim trunks.

Six teenagers cluster at the top, bare feet on warm concrete.

PERPETUA "PETE" ADEYEMI-LANDRY, 16, tall and laconic, stretches her calves against the rail. She does not look down.

BRIAN "BEANPOLE" OKONJO, 15, adjusts his goggles for the fifth time, lips moving as he rehearses a joke he will never tell.

SUNITA MARCHETTI-DAS, 16, ponytail wet, picks at a nail.

CASPIAN FROST-NKEMELU, 17, shirtless, tries very hard to look relaxed.

The twins ROSALIND and GWENDOLYN TARASOVA, 15, share a towel and a single earbud each.

Wind moves the surface of the pool like a hand smoothing a bedsheet.

PETE
Who's first?

BEANPOLE
Not me.

SUNITA
Not me.

CASPIAN
I'll go.

PETE
You said that last week.

CASPIAN
And last week I almost did.

The twins exchange a look. Take the earbuds out in unison.

ROSALIND
We'll go together.

GWENDOLYN
(overlapping)
We'll go together.

^

They step to the edge, hands linked. Neither speaks. Below, a lifeguard whistles a lazy tune.

SUNITA
Wait. Wait. Don't.

ROSALIND
Why?

GWENDOLYN
Why?

SUNITA
Because after this, it's September.

Silence. Even the wind seems to pause.

She says it quietly. They hear it anyway.

Pete finally looks down. Her face softens. She sits on the edge of the platform, legs swinging into the sky.

PETE
Then let's make it a good one.

Caspian sits beside her. Beanpole joins them, goggles fogged. Sunita comes last, close enough that their shoulders touch.

The twins look at each other once more. Then, together, they jump.

The splash is distant and silver. The others lean over to watch the ripples spread outward, ring after ring, until they reach the walls.

BEANPOLE
(softly)
Okay. Now me.
